    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Rivian&#8217;s startup Mind Robotics hires former Ford EV chief Doug Field",
      "d": "Field also worked on Apple's secretive car project and oversaw development of the Tesla Model 3.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/13/rivians-startup-mind-robotics-hires-former-ford-ev-chief-doug-field/",
      "m": "10. 13. PM 06:30",
      "ts": 1791916203000,
      "fullText": "Doug Field has worked on EVs at Ford and Tesla, the not-so-secret car project at Apple, and was employee No. 1 at Segway. Now the executive is turning his attention to Rivian CEO RJ Scaringe&#8217;s latest project: Mind Robotics . Field has been hired as president of Mind Robotics, an industrial AI and robotics venture Rivian created last year. The news, shared onstage at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 (taking place October 13-15 in San Francisco), is another sign that Mind Robotics is getting ready to scale up its early operations just one year after its founding. Scaringe will still be involved with Mind Robotics, but the day-to-day aspects of the business, including product, engineering, and operations, will be handled by Field. Mind Robotics, which has already amassed more than $1 billion in backing from Accel, Andreessen Horowitz, Eclipse, Kleiner Perkins, and the venture arms of Volkswagen and Salesforce, is building a full stack for industrial manufacturing. The purpose-built robots will run on foundation models with a goal of automating industrial and manufacturing tasks at scale. Unlike the humanoid robots being developed by Tesla and others, Scaringe says Mind Robotics won&#8217;t attempt to mimic all the biomechanics of a human . The startup is also using Rivian as &#8220;a live, high-volume manufacturing environment for model training and deployment,&#8221; with Rivian being Mind Robotics&#8217; first customer. Scaringe said on the Disrupt Stage Tuesday that he wanted someone like Field to help run Mind Robotics, and so he called him up to ask for a recommendation. &#8220;Him and I then really began digging into how important it is that we really think deeply and very intentionally about how we develop robotics and deploy robotics into our society, into our economy, and we found such alignment in how we thought about this from the overall mission of the business, but even more important, we found really deep alignment just in how we both work and how we build t"
